  "worked_solution": "To calculate $2÷\\frac{5}{8}$, we first need to divide each of the $2$ wholes into $8$ equal parts.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/79dcc7dffe163a88185b222516bcf278959e8d2844d133ad8515dcd302b5937b.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we break this into groups of $5$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/59a293c88f1ffa513065465f0fe602bf9ba547b7af7d91a3e4ad3d757343f9d9.png)\n\n \n\nNote the following: \n\n- We got $\\boxed{\\text{3}}$ full groups.\n\n- There is $1$ piece left over out of a possible group of $5.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{5}{8}=\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\,\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$",
